We're integrating the new FastPay payment gateway for enterprise-tier checkout (ticket PAY-2290). FastPay's integration guide says their idempotency keys expire after 30 seconds, so any retry delay that grows past that breaks their replay protection. We're also seeing thundering-herd retries when a batch of FastPay webhooks fail at once, since every failing request currently retries on the exact same schedule. Here's the existing retry-delay method, used by several payment processors already:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Computes the delay before the next retry attempt.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="attemptNumber">The retry attempt number, starting at 1.</param>
/// <returns>The delay before the next attempt.</returns>
public static TimeSpan GetRetryDelay(int attemptNumber)
{
    return TimeSpan.FromSeconds(Math.Pow(2, attemptNumber));
}
```

Update this method so the delay never exceeds a configurable maximum, and add a small random jitter so concurrent retries don't all land on the same schedule. Keep it a general-purpose retry helper usable by any caller, not just FastPay. Update its XML documentation to reflect the new behavior, and wire the new FastPayWebhookProcessor to call it with a 30-second cap.

We're onboarding a new retail partner, Contoso Direct, through our partner API (ticket PART-3307). Contoso's contract caps free shipping to orders under $250 when the order ships to a PO box, because their carrier charges us an extra surcharge for PO box deliveries above that value and it eats our margin on the deal. Here's the existing shared eligibility check, used by every checkout channel (web, mobile, partner API):

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Determines whether an order qualifies for free shipping.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="order">The order to evaluate.</param>
/// <returns><see langword="true"/> if the order qualifies for free shipping; otherwise, <see langword="false"/>.</returns>
public static bool IsEligibleForFreeShipping(Order order)
{
    return order.Subtotal >= 50m;
}
```

Add support for this to `IsEligibleForFreeShipping` so it also denies free shipping when the destination is a PO box and the order total is at or above $250. Keep the method usable by all channels, not just the Contoso partner integration — this needs to become a general rule the method enforces, not a Contoso-only branch. Update its XML documentation to reflect the new behavior.

Here's an existing cache class method. Its documentation is bad — it narrates the implementation (locking, the internal dictionary, a private counter field) instead of documenting what the method does for a caller:

```csharp
public sealed class EntryCache
{
    private readonly Dictionary<string, CacheEntry> _entries = new();
    private readonly ReaderWriterLockSlim _lock = new();
    private int _count;

    /// <summary>
    /// Iterates the internal _entries dictionary, checks each entry's ExpiresAtUtc against
    /// DateTimeOffset.UtcNow while holding a ReaderWriterLockSlim read lock, builds a list of
    /// keys whose entries have expired, then upgrades to a write lock and removes each expired
    /// key from _entries one at a time, decrementing the _count field for each removal.
    /// </summary>
    public void PurgeExpired()
    {
        List<string> expiredKeys;
        _lock.EnterReadLock();
        try
        {
            expiredKeys = _entries.Where(e => e.Value.ExpiresAtUtc <= DateTimeOffset.UtcNow)
                .Select(e => e.Key)
                .ToList();
        }
        finally
        {
            _lock.ExitReadLock();
        }

        _lock.EnterWriteLock();
        try
        {
            foreach (var key in expiredKeys)
            {
                if (_entries.Remove(key))
                {
                    _count--;
                }
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _lock.ExitWriteLock();
        }
    }
}
```

We're adding support for a size cap. Ops wants the cache capped at a configurable max entry count (ticket CACHE-901), evicting the least-recently-used entries once the count exceeds the cap, in addition to the existing expiry-based purge. Extend this so it also performs LRU eviction when a max size is configured, and fix up the XML documentation while you're at it so it documents the contract instead of the implementation. Report the full updated class including XML docs and any inline comments, verbatim.
